## FIELD 3 — Abstract/Summary  (limit: 10,000 characters)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

```
Hill flash floods in India — Kedarnath 2013, Chamoli 2021, Sikkim GLOF 2023 — strike villages with minutes of warning or none at all. Existing systems are too coarse: GloFAS runs on ~11 km grid cells, FFGS issues only district-level warnings, and current tools use rainfall alone while ignoring soil saturation and slope. Yet India already operates thousands of ground stations: IMD runs 1,000+ Automatic Weather Stations and ~1,400 Automatic Rain Gauges, CWC issues forecasts at ~338 flood-forecast sites, and hourly river telemetry is published openly on India-WRIS/NWIC. The sensing layer exists — the missing link is integration and village-level intelligence, not new hardware.

JAL-DARPAAN is a complete software platform that closes this gap: village-level flash flood alerts delivered 1–3 hours before disaster strikes, built entirely on India's existing station network, free satellite data, and open-source AI.

WHAT IT DOES
• Fuses 6+ free data sources — IMD station and gridded rainfall, satellite rainfall (GPM IMERG), NASA SMAP soil moisture, 30 m terrain/slope (Copernicus DEM), Sentinel-1 radar, GSI landslide records, plus live CWC/WRIS river levels
• AI predicts the next 1–6 hours — LSTM/GRU models nowcast rain and soil saturation; LightGBM classifies village-level flood risk
• Village/ward-level risk map — every village colored green/yellow/orange/red, refreshed every 15–30 minutes using 30 m micro-watershed mapping
• Actionable alerts, not just warnings — SMS/Telegram/push in Hindi and local languages with village name, flood arrival time, nearest safe shelter, evacuation route, and NDRF helpline
• Explainable AI — every alert shows WHY it fired ("Soil 92% saturated + 45 mm/hr rain → HIGH risk") via SHAP, so officials trust and act on it
• Multi-source confirmation rule — HIGH/CRITICAL alerts fire only when at least 2 independent sources agree, cutting false alarms
• Runs on a 100% free/open-source stack — ₹0 software and hosting cost, replicable by any hill state

SENSOR STRATEGY — AUGMENT, DON'T DUPLICATE
India's Hydrological Information System already contains thousands of ground stations. Our first step is to research, audit, and integrate them. New low-cost ESP32 sensor nodes (~₹1,500/node) are deployed only at villages where the audit shows a genuine coverage gap. A high-fidelity sensor simulator sends data in the exact MQTT JSON format of a real ESP32 node, so the AI is trained and demonstrated today — and real nodes later plug in with zero code change.

WHY IT IS UNIQUE
No operational village-level flash flood warning system exists in India today. Existing global systems (GloFAS ≈ 11 km grid, Google Flood Hub, FFGS) stop at district or major-river level. JAL-DARPAAN is first to deliver hill micro-watershed, village-level risk with explainable, multi-source-confirmed alerts — and it makes government's existing station investments finally save lives instead of sitting siloed across portals.

FEASIBILITY
Every tool is proven open-source; identical models were published in 2024–25 peer-reviewed papers (ConvLSTM ~18% better than LSTM; multi-source fusion cuts false alarms 30%+; LightGBM best speed-accuracy balance for flood risk). All data sources are free and open — satellite feeds and India-WRIS telemetry run the platform end-to-end, so it never depends on a single portal. Operations cost ₹0/month on free-tier hosting. The platform is validated by backtesting against Kedarnath 2013, Chamoli 2021, and Sikkim 2023 before anyone acts on a live alert.

IMPACT
Villagers get 1–3 hours to move family, livestock, and documents instead of minutes of no warning. Officials see which village, when, how bad, and where to go — not a vague district advisory. NDRF pre-positions teams and boats before roads wash out. Primary users: village heads, district disaster managers (DDMA/SDMA), NDRF/SDRF teams, and 10,000+ residents of pilot villages, scaling to 11 Himalayan hill states and Western Ghats districts. The system directly serves NDMA's National Disaster Management Plan and the Sendai Framework, Digital India, and SDG 11.5 and 13.1.

JAL-DARPAAN turns India's existing sensors, free satellite data, and open-source AI into one guardian watching over every hill village — warning in time, explaining itself, and telling people exactly where to go.
```

